# startup - a script run at every shell start (also after `exit`),
# before /etc/crontab's @boot jobs and the prompt. Its output shows on
# the screen; it may ask questions (read, choice). Ctrl-C stops it.
# Holding BOOTSEL while the board starts skips it (safe mode).
# For things that should start once per boot, quietly and logged,
# use @boot lines in /etc/crontab instead.
#
# Examples (remove the # to use):
# echo Welcome to $(uname)
# date
# if exist /root/notes.txt then
#     cat /root/notes.txt
# fi
